 "top": [["\"", -0.0002121554280165583], [" mode", -9.6073637008667]]}, {"t": " mode", "lp": -0.0019866875372827053, "top": [[" mode", -0.0019866875372827053], [" choice", -6.2965497970581055]]}, {"t": " is", "lp": -0.5763416290283203, "top": [[" is", -0.5763416290283203], [" depends", -0.9407634735107422]]}, {"t": " workload", "lp": -0.1105642318725586, "top": [[" workload", -0.1105642318725586], [" highly", -3.0171728134155273]]}, {"t": "-dependent", "lp": -0.0009299667435698211, "top": [["-dependent", -0.0009299667435698211], ["-specific", -7.0683088302612305]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.0005073642241768539, "top": [["\n\n", -0.0005073642241768539], [" if", -9.036456108093262]]}, {"t": "Would", "lp": -0.7422544360160828, "top": [["Would", -0.7422544360160828], ["Do", -1.9364186525344849]]}, {"t": " you", "lp": -0.0014979131519794464, "top": [[" you", -0.0014979131519794464], [" like", -7.098124027252197]]}, {"t": " like", "lp": -0.00027093981043435633, "top": [[" like", -0.00027093981043435633], [" let", -9.852545738220215]]}, {"t": " more", "lp": -0.38434091210365295, "top": [[" more", -0.38434091210365295], [" me", -1.6174551248550415]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing / preemption-based**\n- CCS contexts can be scheduled on the same physical contexts as GCS (Graphics Compute Slices)\n- Uses context preemption to switch between contexts\n- More flexible resource usage\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Dedicated CCS contexts**\n- Each CCS gets its own dedicated context\n- No context stealing between CCS and GCS\n- More predictable isolation between contexts\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **CCS bypass / direct scheduling**\n- CCS contexts bypass the normal scheduling path and are scheduled directly\n- Lower overhead for CCS workloads\n- Bypasses certain queuing mechanisms\n\n## When to use each\n\n**Default behavior:** Mode 1 is typically the default and works well for general use.\n\n**Use Mode 2 when:**\n- You need guaranteed isolation between compute contexts\n- Running mixed graphics and compute workloads where predictability matters\n\n**Use Mode 4 when:**\n- You're running **pure compute workloads** (e.g., AI/ML inference, HPC)\n- You want to minimize scheduling overhead for compute tasks\n- CCS resources aren't being shared with graphics contexts\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** These parameters are somewhat driver-internal and their exact semantics can shift between driver versions. The naming and behavior of these modes has been known to change. I'd recommend:\n\n1. Checking your specific driver version's documentation\n2. Testing with `dmesg` to see how the driver reports the chosen mode\n3. Benchmarking your actual workload, since the \"best\" mode is workload-dependent\n\nWould you like more"}